WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:07.418
Aver helps a team review a change to an agent pipeline.
The change could involve a model, prompt, tool,

00:00:07.418 --> 00:00:13.832
or harness. This walkthrough starts on the testing
website, then follows a free workspace and the paid

00:00:13.832 --> 00:00:16.850
plans using a local demonstration with synthetic
evidence.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:24.078
Start with recorded cases from your own workflow. Compare
the current and proposed configurations, apply

00:00:24.078 --> 00:00:31.024
rules a person approved, and preserve an eligible result.
Aver checks the evidence you submit. It does

00:00:31.024 --> 00:00:33.883
not automatically watch all your production traffic.

00:00:34.983 --> 00:00:41.536
The full loop begins in your own agent harness. Run the
current and candidate configurations on the same

00:00:41.536 --> 00:00:47.360
tasks, repeat the runs, and record the action facts. Aver
imports that evidence and applies human-

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:54.276
approved rules. A pass can become an eligible signed
result; a block or refusal tells the team what needs

00:00:54.276 --> 00:00:57.917
attention. Only an eligible record can support a release-
time gate.

00:00:59.017 --> 00:01:05.417
The documentation explains the complete loop and the
evidence formats. A workflow is saved as an App. A

00:01:05.417 --> 00:01:11.106
checkpoint names the decision being evaluated. A check
applies the policy, and a signed record preserves

00:01:11.106 --> 00:01:12.883
the result and its limits.

00:01:13.983 --> 00:01:20.213
The public Console demonstrates the human judgment
workflow. A reviewer inspects a case and marks the

00:01:20.213 --> 00:01:26.443
observed action as acceptable, unacceptable, or a never
event. Here we make a demonstration judgment and

00:01:26.443 --> 00:01:32.283
undo it. Real policy judgments require a person to
understand the case and the consequences.

00:01:33.383 --> 00:01:39.925
The AI-assist view offers proposals for a person to
review. The public demo contains prepared examples,

00:01:39.925 --> 00:01:46.466
not a fresh live judge call. In the supported customer
workflow, confirming, rejecting, or overriding a

00:01:46.466 --> 00:01:53.008
proposal records what the person decided. Assistance does
not grant a model authority to approve the

00:01:53.008 --> 00:01:53.417
policy.

00:01:54.517 --> 00:02:00.311
In the single-attempt judgment workflow, the tune and
document views show the suggested trade-offs and

00:02:00.311 --> 00:02:06.491
policy. A fitted preference is a suggestion for a person
to review. Our repeated-run example uses

00:02:06.491 --> 00:02:12.672
explicit action rules instead. These are distinct
evidence paths, and the policy version tells a reviewer

00:02:12.672 --> 00:02:14.217
which method was used.

00:02:15.317 --> 00:02:21.921
The policy document gathers the suggested trade-offs,
evidence counts, hard limits, and scope. This is

00:02:21.921 --> 00:02:28.086
the human-judgment path. In the repeated-run workflow
shown next, acceptance comes from explicit action

00:02:28.086 --> 00:02:34.250
rules. A reviewer needs to understand the policy and
method before approving a version.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:41.797
Free includes one actively checked workflow and twenty
five checks per month. The site lists Pro at forty

00:02:41.797 --> 00:02:48.245
nine dollars per month with two hundred fifty checks, and
Team from two hundred forty nine dollars with

00:02:48.245 --> 00:02:53.976
three workflows and one thousand checks. Paid checkout is
not currently configured on the deployed site.

00:02:53.976 --> 00:02:56.483
Later scenes demonstrate the non-charge test flow.

00:02:57.583 --> 00:03:05.051
A new user starts with account creation and the required
terms. An existing user signs in. For the rest

00:03:05.051 --> 00:03:11.339
of this recording, we use the application’s local demo
session and disposable data. No real account

00:03:11.339 --> 00:03:14.483
registration, payment, or customer deployment is being
claimed.

00:03:15.583 --> 00:03:22.324
First create a workspace. It is the boundary for Apps,
policies, evidence, members, and billing. Our

00:03:22.324 --> 00:03:29.486
example is a workspace maintenance agent, where the team
wants to check that a proposed version respects

00:03:29.486 --> 00:03:30.750
file access boundaries.

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:38.803
We name the workflow Workspace Maintenance Agent and
identify who will use the decision. This creates the

00:03:38.803 --> 00:03:45.347
App that will hold our checkpoint, comparisons, and
records. The same structure works for other agent

00:03:45.347 --> 00:03:46.983
pipelines and consequential actions.

00:03:48.083 --> 00:03:54.060
Choose the first comparison and create the App. A
checkpoint focuses the review on a particular decision.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:04:00.389
In this example, the recorded action is a file write
outside the allowed scope. We will compare current

00:04:00.389 --> 00:04:02.850
and candidate behavior on the same tasks.

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:10.179
Billing shows that this workspace is on Free. The
allowance is attached to the workspace and its actively

00:04:10.179 --> 00:04:16.407
checked Apps. You can inspect usage and retention before
choosing a paid plan. We will complete the core

00:04:16.407 --> 00:04:18.483
evidence and record workflow here first.

00:04:19.583 --> 00:04:25.833
The repeated-run workflow starts with action facts,
rather than asking a judge to score every transcript.

00:04:25.833 --> 00:04:32.472
Here we import eighty synthetic tasks, five attempts per
side: eight hundred rows in total. We explicitly

00:04:32.472 --> 00:04:37.550
mark the source as synthetic and retain hashes instead of
raw case text.

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:45.361
The preview checks the structure before import. Building
the bench saves the task evidence and the exact

00:04:45.361 --> 00:04:51.676
comparison identities. These are recorded synthetic
outcomes; this import does not run a live model or

00:04:51.676 --> 00:04:53.650
reconstruct a customer’s entire pipeline.

00:04:54.750 --> 00:05:01.776
For repeated runs, a person approves explicit action
rules. This policy treats firing write outside scope

00:05:01.776 --> 00:05:08.362
as harmful. It uses a five percent tolerance, requires
consistent attempts before assigning a typical

00:05:08.362 --> 00:05:14.949
decision, and refuses excessively unstable evidence. The
draft was prepared through the API; here the

00:05:14.949 --> 00:05:17.583
owner reviews its actual approval document.

00:05:18.683 --> 00:05:24.806
Approval activates this exact version for this
checkpoint. That is a human action. An agent token cannot

00:05:24.806 --> 00:05:30.569
approve the policy. The approval document makes the
method and limits visible, so a reviewer can

00:05:30.569 --> 00:05:33.450
understand what a future pass would actually mean.

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:41.061
Here is an alternative synthetic candidate under the same
five percent rule. Five of sixty tasks reliably

00:05:41.061 --> 00:05:47.189
switch toward the harmful action. The uncertainty
straddles the limit, so Aver refuses to decide. The

00:05:47.189 --> 00:05:54.083
next-actions list names the tasks that need review. A
refusal cannot be saved as a signed passing record.

00:05:55.183 --> 00:06:00.899
This second alternative makes the harmful action on
twenty four of eighty tasks. That exceeds the same

00:06:00.899 --> 00:06:06.615
approved limit with enough evidence to block. The owner
can inspect the changed cases and fix the

00:06:06.615 --> 00:06:10.650
candidate. We do not loosen the rule to make the
demonstration pass.

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:17.405
The candidate passes on these eighty synthetic tasks
under the approved limits. The readout separates

00:06:17.405 --> 00:06:23.437
reliable decision changes from changes in action
frequency. A pass is scoped to these inputs and

00:06:23.437 --> 00:06:27.583
assumptions. It does not prove that the agent is
universally safe.

00:06:28.683 --> 00:06:35.719
An eligible verdict can be saved as a signed record. The
record binds the comparison, evidence scope,

00:06:35.719 --> 00:06:41.928
policy, and result. Because our evidence is synthetic,
this is a signed demonstration. It cannot

00:06:41.928 --> 00:06:43.583
authorize a real release.

00:06:44.683 --> 00:06:51.243
The record page gives a reviewer the policy, evidence
scope, result, and limitations together. A

00:06:51.243 --> 00:06:56.490
cryptographic signature establishes integrity. Human
approval is a separate fact. Reviewers should

00:06:56.490 --> 00:07:03.050
inspect both, together with the record’s current status
and the relevance of the underlying evidence.

00:07:04.150 --> 00:07:10.410
The owner can append a review decision and a comment.
This preserves who reviewed the result without

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:15.933
rewriting the signed credential. Here we approve only the
synthetic demonstration. That review does not

00:07:15.933 --> 00:07:19.983
turn demonstration data into customer evidence or grant
permission to release.

00:07:21.083 --> 00:07:28.083
A reviewer can open a holder-shared verification page
without an account. The page separates signature

00:07:28.083 --> 00:07:33.683
authenticity, current status, human review, and recorded
scope. This demonstration remains clearly

00:07:33.683 --> 00:07:39.750
labeled synthetic and non-deployable. Verification does
not prove that the original measurements were

00:07:39.750 --> 00:07:41.150
truthful or representative.

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:48.426
The verification page shows four separate facts:
authenticity, current status, human review, and the

00:07:48.426 --> 00:07:55.043
scope covered by the evidence. Our synthetic result is
authentic under the local development key,

00:07:55.043 --> 00:08:01.660
current, and reviewed. It still cannot authorize a
release. That distinction remains visible after a

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:02.983
human approves it.

00:08:04.083 --> 00:08:10.424
Now we demonstrate the paid experience. The local
application uses its non-charge checkout path, so no

00:08:10.424 --> 00:08:17.161
card is collected and no money moves. The workspace
changes from Free to Pro. The displayed allowance

00:08:17.161 --> 00:08:21.917
rises to two hundred fifty monthly checks and ninety day
evidence-detail retention.

00:08:23.017 --> 00:08:29.033
Team adds capacity for multiple actively checked
workflows, longer retention, team roles, and audit

00:08:29.033 --> 00:08:35.909
history. This upgrade is also synthetic test billing.
Extra comparison checkpoints inside one App do not

00:08:35.909 --> 00:08:38.917
each count as a separate billed workflow.

00:08:40.017 --> 00:08:46.620
The rechecks tab exposes cadence and budget controls
together with readiness. This App does not yet have

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:52.446
the completed model-change Check required by this
scheduling path, so its controls are disabled. Paid

00:08:52.446 --> 00:08:57.883
capacity does not remove that requirement. No recurring
job is enabled in this recording.

00:08:58.983 --> 00:09:04.607
Workspace settings bring membership and operational
controls into one place. Roles determine what a

00:09:04.607 --> 00:09:11.035
collaborator may do. Audit history helps the team review
important actions. The demo does not send

00:09:11.035 --> 00:09:14.650
invitations to real people or change a customer
workspace.

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:22.148
An App-scoped token can submit evidence, run supported
checks, read verdicts, save eligible records, and

00:09:22.148 --> 00:09:28.545
export configuration when every release requirement is
met. The token cannot approve policies or add

00:09:28.545 --> 00:09:34.517
human signoff. Here we show the permission setup without
displaying or issuing a secret.

00:09:35.617 --> 00:09:41.521
The App’s agent access page generates setup instructions
for the selected checkpoint. Choose an agent

00:09:41.521 --> 00:09:47.818
client, store the limited token in a secret reference,
and use the generated configuration. An optional

00:09:47.818 --> 00:09:54.115
local runner keeps model credentials and execution on
your compute. This scene shows the setup; no

00:09:54.115 --> 00:09:56.083
external agent is connected here.

00:09:57.183 --> 00:10:03.779
The agent guide documents the integration sequence. Your
pipeline produces evidence, submits it for its

00:10:03.779 --> 00:10:11.255
App, runs a supported check, reads the result, and saves
an eligible record. Human review stays outside

00:10:11.255 --> 00:10:16.972
the machine’s permissions. Exact method compatibility
matters; supported operations do not imply that

00:10:16.972 --> 00:10:20.050
every evidence format runs through every endpoint.

00:10:21.150 --> 00:10:27.887
The CI guide explains the release-time check. Bind the
App, checkpoint, and immutable record explicitly.

00:10:27.887 --> 00:10:34.624
The gate also considers eligibility and status. A
synthetic demonstration like ours must remain blocked

00:10:34.624 --> 00:10:38.217
for release, even though its statistical verdict passed.

00:10:39.317 --> 00:10:45.771
Fleet is the overview of the team’s workflows. Console is
where evidence and policy are examined.

00:10:45.771 --> 00:10:51.822
Decisions focuses on candidate comparisons, and Records
preserves saved results. The daily loop is to

00:10:51.822 --> 00:10:58.277
update the evidence, check a change under approved rules,
review the result, and preserve what was

00:10:58.277 --> 00:10:59.083
actually decided.

00:11:00.183 --> 00:11:08.088
The workflow ends with a result people and systems can
inspect. This demo covered the public site, the

00:11:08.088 --> 00:11:14.236
free core workflow, paid test entitlements, team
controls, and agent integration setup. All workspace

00:11:14.236 --> 00:11:19.944
data was synthetic. Live payment, production release
approval, and scheduled execution were not

00:11:19.944 --> 00:11:20.383
performed.
